<sn><md>### Anion Exchange in Soil

**Definition:**
Anion exchange is the reversible process in which negatively charged ions (anions) held on positively charged sites of soil particles are replaced by other anions from the soil solution.

**Reaction:**

$$
\text{Soil} - \text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^- + \text{NO}_3^- \rightleftharpoons \text{Soil} - \text{NO}_3^- + \text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^-
$$

**Origin of Positive Charge:**
- **Protonation of –OH groups** on clay edges and Fe/Al oxide surfaces at low pH:
$$
\text{Fe} - \text{OH} + \text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{Fe} - \text{OH}_2^+
$$
- **Broken edges** of clay minerals
- **Iron and aluminum oxides** (goethite, gibbsite) — main source in acidic soils
- **Allophane** in volcanic ash soils

**Anion Exchange Capacity (AEC):**
The total exchangeable anions a soil can hold. It is **much lower than CEC** in most soils because negative charge dominates. AEC increases at **low pH** and in **oxide-rich soils**.

**Selectivity Series:**
$$
\text{OH}^- &gt; \text{H}_2\text{PO}_4^- &gt; \text{SO}_4^{2-} &gt; \text{Cl}^- &gt; \text{NO}_3^-
$$

**Types:**
- **Exchangeable (non-specific):** Cl⁻, NO₃⁻ — weakly held, easily leached
- **Specifically adsorbed:** Phosphate, sulfate — held by ligand exchange, less reversible

**Factors Affecting:**
- **pH:** Low pH → more positive charge → more AEC
- **Fe/Al oxide content:** High oxides → higher AEC
- **Clay type:** Kaolinite and oxides &gt; smectite
- **Competing anions:** OH⁻ and phosphate displace others

**Importance:**
- **Phosphate retention:** Reduces leaching but can fix phosphate, lowering availability
- **Nitrate leaching:** Weakly held NO₃⁻ pollutes groundwater
- **Sulfate retention:** Strongly held in acidic soils
- **Liming:** Raises pH, reduces AEC, releases adsorbed anions

**Example:**
In acidic red soils rich in iron oxides, phosphate is strongly retained by anion exchange and ligand exchange, making it less available to plants.

**Conclusion:**
Anion exchange is the reversible replacement of anions on positively charged soil sites. It is pH-dependent and important in acidic, oxide-rich soils, controlling the mobility of phosphate, sulfate, nitrate, and chloride.</md></sn>